  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nOne more very pleasant day today with highs in the upper 70s to mid \n80s, with low relative humidity, and light winds. Outside of some \nthin smoke from wildfires, skies are expected to be mostly clear \ntoday and tonight. Good news for those of you who wish to catch a \nglimpse of the partial Lunar eclipse tonight. With one caveat.... \nThose in south central South Dakota have a low chance (<30%) of \nshowers and thunderstorms this afternoon and into the evening. A \nsubtle short wave and weak  to mid-level convergence may work \ntogether to trigger convection. MUCAPE values range widely between \nshort term guidance, from 500 to nearly 2000 J/kg and a moderately \nstrong cap will be in place. However, steep low-level lapse rates \nand 35 kts of 0-6 bulk shear could support a stronger updraft or \ntwo. Risks with stronger storms is damaging wind gusts to 60 mph and \nmarginally severe hail to 1 inch in diameter. Lows for tonight will \nfall to the 50s and low 60s. \n\nFriday a few scattered storms may linger along an area of weak mid-\nlevel convergence. These should cease by late morning. Then we will \nsee the return of higher relative humidity as southerly winds usher \nin a rich Theta-E ridge. Highs are expected to climb to the 80s with \na few 90s sprinkled in. To the west we see a stronger mid-level wave \nand associated surface low pressure pushing east out of the Rockies. \nThis will work to tighten the SPG and induce a strong LLJ through \nthe afternoon. With good mixing we'll see gusts along and west of I-\n29 in the 25-35 mph range. South central South Dakota will see the \nhighest gusts. To the east of I-29 gusts will be 25 mph or less. As \nthe low pushes closer a warm front will drift north through the CWA, \npossibly triggering showers and thunderstorms in the mid to late \nafternoon. Through the remainder of the afternoon and evening the \nsystem will become more compact. The LLJ ramps up rapidly through \nthe evening and WAA intensifies which could potentially expand storm \ncoverage and severity around midnight. A marginally unstable \nenvironment will be in place with MUCAPE values of 1000-2000 J/kg, \nmid-level lapse rates of 7 C/km or higher, and 35-45 kts of 0-6 bulk \nshear. There remains a couple of factors that keep confidence in \nstorms low. While marginally unstable, soundings indicate a strong \ncap will be in place across most of the region. Without stronger \nforcing it is uncertain if convection will be able to initiate. \nHowever, should storms form they have the potential to rapidly \nbecome strong to severe. Risks include large hail to 2 inches in \ndiameter and wind gusts to 60 mph. \n\nStorms will continue into Saturday morning before pushing east of \nthe region. The rest of Saturday looks to be dry, warm, and humid \nwith highs climbing into the mid 80s to mid 90s, warmest over south \ncentral South Dakota. Heat Index values will be very near to \ncritical (100 F) through the James River Valley. Sunday will be very \nsimilar, with the 90s expanded east into northwest Iowa. Heat Index \nvalues will once again be very near to critical. We will continue to \nmonitor these trends. Sunday afternoon a cold front will move \nthrough the region, possibly bringing another round of showers and \nthunderstorms. \n\nFor the first half of next week the upper ridge we've been under \nwill be squashed somewhat by a strong upper low over central Canada. \nFlow remains southwest with multiple short waves traversing through. \nThis will keep shower and thunderstorm chances in the forecast \nthrough at least Wednesday.",
